            <ArticleTitle>Penile metastasis from prostate cancer: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e14-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1700/1430.15830</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Metastatic involvement of the penis is rare. About 80% of secondary lesions originate from pelvic primary tumors, mainly bladder and prostate. We present a case of prostatic mucinous adenocarcinoma with penile metastasis symptomatic for pain, which was treated with external-beam radiation (35 Gy/14 fractions; 2.5 Gy daily) combined with androgen deprivation, resulting in complete pain relief and objective response after treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
